mastery.solution_text:
A square is a parallelogram, so its diagonals bisect each other: $AE = CE$ and $BE = DE$.
In $\triangle ABE$ and $\triangle CBE$:
$AB = CB$ (sides of a square are equal)
$AE = CE$ (diagonals of a square bisect each other)
$BE$ is common.
$\therefore \triangle ABE \cong \triangle CBE$ (SSS)
$\therefore \angle AEB = \angle CEB$ (matching angles of congruent triangles)
$\angle AEB + \angle CEB = 180^{\circ}$ (angles on a straight line $AC$)
$\therefore \angle AEB = \angle CEB = 90^{\circ}$
So the diagonals of a square bisect each other at right angles.
